{"name":"French Pass","hex":"#a4d2e0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"french","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To prepare food by cutting it into strips.","To kiss (another person) while inserting one’s tongue into the other person's mouth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/french"},{"term":"pass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To change place.\n To move or be moved from one place to another.","To move or be moved from one place to another."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An opening, road, or track, available for passing; especially, one through or over some dangerous or otherwise impracticable barrier such as a mountain range; a passageway; a defile; a ford.","A channel connecting a river or body of water to the sea, for example at the mouth (delta) of a river."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pass"}],"entries":[{"title":"French Pass","blocks":[{"text":"French Pass is a narrow and treacherous stretch of water that separates D'Urville Island, at the north end of the South Island of New Zealand, from the mainland coast. At one end is Tasman Bay, and at the other end the outer Pelorus Sound / Te Hoiere leads out to Cook Strait."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/FrenchPassFromTheSW.JPG/330px-FrenchPassFromTheSW.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Pass"},{"title":"French Pass (Oregon)","blocks":[{"text":"French Pass is a gap in the mountains of Northeast Oregon, US, within Morrow County in an unpopulated region of the state."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Pass_(Oregon)"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"french":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fb/En-us-French.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/